  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Warmer and more humid conditions return this weekend \nwith at least periodic chances for some showers and scattered \nthunderstorms Saturday afternoon through Sunday night.\n\nHigh pressure will keep dry weather intact through at least midday \nSaturday. The surface high will move east into New England tonight, \nwith a southerly return flow advecting a warmer and more humid \nairmass back in across western and northcentral NY from southwest to \nnortheast through the day Saturday. A more humid airmass in \ncombination with daytime heating may lead to some scattered \ndiurnally driven convection across western NY Saturday afternoon. \nThe highest probability for showers and a few storms during the \nafternoon hours will be across the interior Southern Tier where the \nbest moisture will overlap with somewhat better forcing closer to a \nmid level trough passing by just to our southeast. Chances for \nscattered showers and isolated storms will then spread from \nsouthwest to northeast across the remainder of the area from late \nSaturday afternoon through the first half of Saturday night.\n\nTiming of a cold frontal passage on Sunday remains somewhat in \nquestion, with the overall consensus still advertising quite a wide \nwindow, with a frontal passage sometime mid morning to late \nafternoon Sunday. This will bring the next chance for another round \nof showers and scattered storms along and ahead of the front. \nDeepest moisture will exit east prior to the arrival of the front, \nhowever 0-6 km shear profiles increasing to 30-35 knots along the \nboundary combined with building diurnal instability may be enough to \ntrigger some showers and storms, of which a few isolated stronger \nstorms may have the capability to produce some gusty winds. This \nwill mainly hinge on frontal timing when it comes to better pinning \ndown a more specific area of interest. This has led to the SPC \nissuing a fairly large Day 3 Marginal Risk stretching from portions \nof the Ohio Valley to western New England owed to the frontal timing \nand other convective elements still being in question. We will be \nentering the higher resolution model window over the next 24 hours, \nwhich should help start to make things a bit more clear.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cool and dry weather expected on Monday, then \nturning warmer Tuesday, with the next chance for some unsettled \nweather arriving by midweek.\n\nFast-moving pattern will continue next week with an area of Canadian \nsurface high pressure bringing dry, but very cool conditions to \nstart off the new work week. Will likely see some lake clouds \nsoutheast of the lakes Monday morning, with potentially a few lake \nenhanced rain showers as well with sharp mid and upper level passing \noverhead. Some guidance has 850 mb temps bottoming out around +3C to \n+5C. The high will then quickly shift east with a strengthening \nsoutherly return flow quickly boosting chilly Tuesday morning lows \nback above average by afternoon with mainly dry weather anticipated. \nUnsettled weather is then expected to return, with the potential for \na frontal system to stall out near or over our region, bringing the \npotential for periodic rounds of showers and storms by midweek.",
